 ## Background
-The method originated from the need to perform volume rendering on programmable graphics hardware. Early implementations used fragment programs to simulate ray tracing on consumer graphics boards, as presented by Stegmaier et al. in 2005 [S4]. This approach exploited the parallelism of GPUs to approximate the volume rendering integral [S4]. Later implementations, such as those described in 2017, refined the technique using two-pass rasterization to decouple ray intersection calculation from volumetric integration, allowing for efficient use of OpenGL fragment shaders [S2].
